# Documentation

The docs site is Astro + Starlight, served from `docs/` and deployed to GitHub Pages on merge to `master`. It is
separate from the extension's TypeScript project (the root `tsconfig.json` excludes `docs/`).

```bash
bun run docs:dev     # http://localhost:4321/torntools_extension/
bun run docs:build
```

## Every feature change needs a docs page

When you add a feature, create its page. When you meaningfully change one — new setting, changed behaviour, new
requirement — update its page. Coverage is currently thin (a couple of pages against ~202 features), so in practice most
feature work means writing a page from scratch.

The file goes at `docs/src/content/docs/features/<name>.mdx`, where `<name>` is exactly the feature's folder name under
`src/common/features/`. The sidebar is autogenerated from the directory, so there is no navigation to register.

## Start from the template

Copy `docs/src/content/docs/features/__placeholder.mdx`. Its double-underscore prefix keeps it out of the build, and its
HTML comments are the authoritative spec — delete them as you fill each section in.

```mdx
---
title: Energy Warning
description: Prevents accidentally wasting energy past the 1000E soft cap when using consumable items.
---

import { Aside } from "@astrojs/starlight/components";

One or two sentences on what the feature does for the reader.
```

| Section | Required | Content |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `title` + `description` frontmatter | Yes | `description` is one sentence; it is used for previews and search. |
| Intro paragraph | Yes | 1–2 sentences. An `<Aside>` here only if it genuinely earns its place — most features don't need one. |
| `## How It Works` | Yes | The mechanism, in numbered steps where there is a sequence. |
| `## Where It Works` | Optional | `Page \| Behavior` table. Skip it when there's one obvious location. |
| `## Usage` | Optional | What it shows and how to drive it — filters, view modes, columns. |
| `## Setup / Requirements` | Optional | Numbered prerequisites (API key, external service). Delete the heading when there are none. |
| `## Settings` | Yes, when the feature has settings | Table of setting path, default, description. |
| `## Troubleshooting` | Yes | `Problem \| Likely Cause` table. Keep the heading and table even when there's little to say. |

Never add an H1 — the title comes from frontmatter. Use `###` for subsections.

## Settings tables

Three columns, the path backticked exactly as it appears in `DEFAULT_STORAGE`, and human-readable defaults rather than
booleans. Verify both against `src/common/utils/data/default-database.ts`; a wrong path is worse than no table. Include
closely related settings in the same table.

```mdx
| Setting                                        | Default | Description                        |
| ---------------------------------------------- | ------- | ---------------------------------- |
| `settings.pages.travel.table`                  | On      | Master toggle for the Travel Table |
| `settings.pages.travel.autoTravelTableCountry` | Off     | Auto-select a country's flag       |
```

## Style

- Write for a Torn player, not a developer: second person, plain language, and explain Torn mechanics where they matter.
  Keep implementation details out unless they change what the user should do.
- Document what the code actually does today. These pages are read as ground truth, so check the feature source rather
  than describing intent — including the awkward inconsistencies (the energy-warning page documents that the Quick Items
  variant behaves differently, which is exactly right).
- `Aside` is the only Starlight component in use. Reach for `Steps`, `Tabs` or `Card` only when a page genuinely needs
  them.
- Blockquotes for text the user sees in-game; bold for page and field names inside tables.
- Wrap prose at a readable width (~120 characters). Oxfmt's 160 applies to code, not MDX prose.

## The AI disclaimer

`docs/src/components/AIDisclaimer.astro` is registered as the global `Footer` override in
[docs/astro.config.mjs](mdc:docs/astro.config.mjs), so the notice already appears on every page. Do **not** import it into
individual pages.

Its existence is also a standing instruction: the disclaimer promises a human reviewed the content. Keep pages factual
and verifiable, and flag anything you were unsure about so it can actually be checked.
